Day 288/300: Find All Possible Stable Binary Arrays I

Problem Statement #3129

Problem Statement Explanation:

You are given three integers zero, one, and limit. 
A binary array is called stable if:
It contains exactly zero occurrences of 0.
It contains exactly one occurrences of 1.
No subarray contains more than limit consecutive elements of the same value.
Return the total number of stable binary arrays. Since the answer may be very large, return it modulo 10^9+7.

Solution Approach:

The challenge is to count arrangements while ensuring no "clumps" of 0s or 1s exceed the limit. Think of this as building a string digit by digit, where each choice is constrained by how many identical digits we just placed.

Solution Approach: Dynamic Programming
We use a 3D Dynamic Programming table dp[i][j][k], where:

i: Number of 0s used so far.
j: Number of 1s used so far.
k: The last digit placed (0 or 1).

State Transitions
Adding a 0: To form dp[i][j][0], we can append a 0 to any stable array that used i-1 zeros and j ones.
dp[i][j][0] = dp[i-1][j][0] + dp[i-1][j][1]

Handling the Limit: If the number of zeros i exceeds limit, we must subtract the cases that became "unstable" by adding that last zero. 
An array becomes unstable only if it already had exactly limit zeros at the end.
The "bad" cases are those where we had i - limit - 1 zeros, then a 1, followed by exactly limit zeros.
Subtraction: dp[i][j][0] = (dp[i][j][0] - dp[i-limit-1][j][1])

Base Cases
Arrays consisting only of 0s (up to limit) are valid: dp[i][0][0] = 1.
Arrays consisting only of 1s (up to limit) are valid: dp[0][j][1] = 1.

Here's the Code:

class Solution {
    public int numberOfStableArrays(int zero, int one, int limit) {
        final int MOD = 1_000_000_007;
        long[][][] dp = new long[zero + 1][one + 1][2];

        for (int i = 0; i <= Math.min(zero, limit); i++) dp[i][0][0] = 1;
        for (int j = 0; j <= Math.min(one, limit); j++) dp[0][j][1] = 1;

        for (int i = 1; i <= zero; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= one; j++) {
                dp[i][j][0] = (dp[i - 1][j][0] + dp[i - 1][j][1]) % MOD;
                if (i > limit) {
                    dp[i][j][0] = (dp[i][j][0] - dp[i - limit - 1][j][1] + MOD) % MOD;
                }

                dp[i][j][1] = (dp[i][j - 1][0] + dp[i][j - 1][1]) % MOD;
                if (j > limit) {
                    dp[i][j][1] = (dp[i][j][1] - dp[i][j - limit - 1][0] + MOD) % MOD;
                }
            }
        }

        return (int) ((dp[zero][one][0] + dp[zero][one][1]) % MOD);
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(1)
Space Complexity: O(1)



